{
  "suite": "money.parse",
  "spec": "0.3.0",
  "description": "Human-facing decimal strings (spec 3.6): strict ASCII; fractional digits beyond the currency's exponent are accepted when every excess digit is zero (the value is preserved exactly) and otherwise rejected unless a rounding mode is given. `minor` is a decimal string so i64 extremes survive JavaScript numbers. Rounding modes are defined in spec 3.6 (down truncates toward zero).",
  "source": "Hand-derived from the ISO 4217 exponents in conformance/money/currency.json and spec 3.2/3.6; no implementation consulted. Method for every expected `minor`: write the input as an optional sign, integer digits I and n fractional digits F, and read the currency's exponent e. With n <= e the magnitude is I followed by F padded on the right to e digits. With n > e, split F into the first e digits (kept) and the remaining n - e (excess); if every excess digit is 0 the magnitude is I followed by kept, otherwise the case is an error unless a rounding mode is supplied. The sign is applied last, and -0 is 0 because minor units are signed 64-bit integers. kes-down-negative: -1029.5 truncated toward zero is -1029 (a floor would give -1030). Value-preserving trailing zeros (ADR 0016, spec 3.6): KES has exponent 2, so \"10.290\" keeps \"29\" with excess \"0\", giving 1029, and \"-10.290\" gives -1029; \"10.2900000\" has excess \"00000\" and gives the same 1029. JPY has exponent 0, so \"5000.00\", \"5000.0\" and \"5000.000000\" all keep nothing and carry excess that is entirely zero, giving 5000, while \"-0.00\" gives 0. BHD has exponent 3, so \"1.2340\" keeps \"234\" with excess \"0\", giving 1234, and \"1.2345\" (bhd-reject-four-places) has excess \"5\" and stays an error. \"10.295\" KES and \"10.2000000001\" KES carry a non-zero excess digit and are errors under reject; \"5000.50\" JPY is the same at exponent 0, and with half_up its excess \"50\" is exactly half a minor unit, a tie that rounds away from zero to 5001. A rounding mode never changes a value-preserving result: \"5000.00\" JPY half_up is 5000 and \"1.2340\" BHD down is 1234, matching reject. Decimal-grammar edges: the decimal input grammar written in spec 3.6 (optional minus, digits, optional dot and digits; leading zeros permitted; negative zero forms parse to 0) is deliberately looser than the wire rule of 3.2, which governs the wire amount only, so \"007\" is the integer 7, i.e. 700 KES minor units, and \"-0.00\" is zero, i.e. 0. half_even and zero at exponent 3: \"10.290\" KES has an all-zero excess, so no rounding decision is taken and no mode may call it a tie; half_even gives the same 1029 as reject even though the kept magnitude 1029 is odd. \"0.0000\" BHD keeps \"000\" with excess \"0\", so the magnitude is 0 and no padding is needed. The i64 boundary through the excess-zeros path: JPY has exponent 0, so the magnitude of \"9223372036854775807.00\" is its integer digits read as base 10, and the expected value is the limit itself - 9223372036854775807 is 2^63 - 1, exactly i64::MAX, so it fits; \"9223372036854775808.00\" is 2^63, one more than i64::MAX, so it does not and is HEPH-MONEY-422; \"-9223372036854775808.00\" has magnitude 2^63 and negates to -2^63, exactly i64::MIN, so it fits.",
